  "configuration_id": "config_002",
  "reason": "Evidence: Our four completed experiments establish that (a) image-only resnet50 is very weak (0.208), (b) RNA-only scvi_n200 is moderate (0.520), and (c) multimodal scvi_n200+resnet50 with concat fusion is the best so far (0.536), with imagenet_vit_l offering negligible improvement (0.532). All tested configurations use singleprofile; the multiprofile dimension is completely unexplored. Question: Does aggregating multiple profiles (via avgpool) improve or degrade performance relative to the singleprofile baseline? config_002 (multimodal, multiprofile, scvi_n200, resnet50, avgpool, concat) is the minimal-change extension of our best result config_038—only the profile setting changes from single to multi, with avgpool as a simple, interpretable aggregation. This cleanly isolates the profile-setting effect. Why this is the best next experiment: Under a limited budget, resolving whether multiprofile is beneficial or harmful is the highest-uncertainty, highest-impact design question remaining. If multiprofile helps, it opens a rich sub-space (aggregation type, encoder choice) to exploit; if it hurts, we can prune many configurations and focus on singleprofile refinements. A singleprofile fusion-method test (e.g., weighted vs concat) would be a smaller, lower-uncertainty question by comparison."
